WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.641
An Orchard Core application can host many separate websites.

00:00:04.641 --> 00:00:06.131
Each one is a tenant.

00:00:06.131 --> 00:00:09.937
In this video, you will learn what a tenant is, how the Tenants

00:00:09.937 --> 00:00:14.130
module is organized, and how to read the list of tenants in the admin.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:22.115
A tenant is one website hosted by the application.

00:00:22.115 --> 00:00:27.132
Tenants keep their content, themes, and users apart, each in its own database

00:00:27.132 --> 00:00:29.851
or its own tables of a shared database.

00:00:29.851 --> 00:00:32.390
A single deployment serves them all.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.937
Every application has a tenant named Default.

00:00:35.937 --> 00:00:40.435
It is the first tenant you set up, and the only one that manages the others:

00:00:40.435 --> 00:00:43.617
the Tenants pages exist on the Default tenant alone.

00:00:43.617 --> 00:00:46.030
The Default tenant cannot be disabled.

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:51.383
A request reaches a tenant through its host name, its URL prefix, or both.

00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:55.180
One tenant can answer on a host name of its own, and another

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:59.380
under the first segment of the path, such as a prefix named bakery.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.496
A tenant is in one state at a time.

00:01:02.496 --> 00:01:06.824
Uninitialized means it exists but is not set up, and Running means it

00:01:06.824 --> 00:01:08.960
is set up and answers requests.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.230
Disabled means it is set up but stopped, and Enable starts it again.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.211
The Tenants module groups several features.

00:01:20.211 --> 00:01:26.170
The one named Tenants adds the Multi-Tenancy menu, where you manage the tenants of the application.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.461
Tenant Feature Profiles limits the features that each tenant can use.

00:01:31.461 --> 00:01:38.424
Distributed Tenants keeps tenant states in sync, and needs a distributed cache and a stateless configuration.

00:01:38.424 --> 00:01:42.420
Like Tenants, both are available only on the Default tenant.

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:47.666
Static File Provider serves static files from a folder of each tenant, so

00:01:47.666 --> 00:01:50.625
two tenants can have files with the same name.

00:01:50.625 --> 00:01:54.040
You enable it on each tenant that needs its own files.

00:01:54.790 --> 00:02:02.455
Managing tenants requires the Manage tenants permission, and managing feature profiles requires Manage tenant feature profiles.

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:05.970
The Administrator role has both permissions by default.

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:14.851
Let's open Tools, then Features, on the Default tenant, and search for tenants.

00:02:14.851 --> 00:02:19.043
The features of the module are among the results, grouped by category.

00:02:19.503 --> 00:02:22.734
Tenants is listed under the Infrastructure category.

00:02:22.734 --> 00:02:24.183
Let's select Enable.

00:02:24.651 --> 00:02:28.997
The page confirms that the feature was enabled, and the admin menu shows

00:02:28.997 --> 00:02:31.951
a Multi-Tenancy section with Tenants inside it.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:40.449
Multi-Tenancy, Tenants lists every tenant of the application.

00:02:40.449 --> 00:02:45.250
Each row shows the tenant's name, with badges for its state and, when

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:49.347
they are set, its category, database provider, and recipe.

00:02:49.347 --> 00:02:53.060
The description and host name, if any, appear below.

00:02:53.519 --> 00:02:57.960
The search box finds tenants by name, host name, or URL prefix.

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:02.337
The Category, State, and Sort menus narrow and order the list.

00:03:02.337 --> 00:03:06.649
Selecting a state badge shows only the tenants in that state.

00:03:07.149 --> 00:03:11.460
To act on several tenants at once, select their checkboxes.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:16.699
The Actions menu then offers Disable and Enable for the selected tenants.

00:03:20.966 --> 00:03:24.345
The buttons of a row depend on the state of the tenant.

00:03:24.345 --> 00:03:27.225
The Default tenant has Edit, View, and Reload.

00:03:27.225 --> 00:03:31.546
It has no Disable button, because the Default tenant cannot be disabled.

00:03:32.043 --> 00:03:36.011
A Running tenant such as Bakery adds Disable and Features.

00:03:36.011 --> 00:03:40.661
Features manages the features of that tenant from the Default tenant.

00:03:40.661 --> 00:03:46.613
View opens the site of the tenant, which the same application serves under its URL prefix.

00:03:47.079 --> 00:03:52.749
A Disabled tenant such as Studio has Enable instead of View, Disable, and Features.

00:03:52.749 --> 00:03:58.802
An Uninitialized tenant such as Workshop has Setup instead, which opens its setup screen.

00:03:58.802 --> 00:04:04.409
Remove appears only for these two states, and only when tenant removal is allowed.

00:04:08.609 --> 00:04:10.647
Add Tenant opens the form.

00:04:10.647 --> 00:04:14.786
The Name identifies the tenant and cannot be changed later.

00:04:14.786 --> 00:04:20.389
Category and Description are shown in the list, and the category is also a filter.

00:04:20.844 --> 00:04:25.395
Url Prefix and Hostname decide which requests the tenant answers.

00:04:25.395 --> 00:04:29.947
The prefix is a single path segment, and no two tenants can share

00:04:29.947 --> 00:04:31.934
the same host and prefix.

00:04:32.394 --> 00:04:37.107
The Recipe and database settings preset the setup screen of the tenant.

00:04:37.107 --> 00:04:42.921
Create saves the tenant as Uninitialized, and Create & Setup also opens its setup screen.

00:04:42.921 --> 00:04:45.614
Cancel leaves the form without saving.

00:04:47.614 --> 00:04:53.758
Tenants let one application host many separate sites, and you manage them from the Default tenant.

00:04:53.758 --> 00:04:55.294
Thanks for watching.

